--TEST--
Core\Uri::decodeComponent and decodeFormValue read `%20` alike and `+` differently, either case of hex reads, and a malformed escape is text — spec § 12
--FILE--
<?mwl
// The mirror of the encoding pair: a `+` is a literal `+` to the component
// decoder and a space to the form one. This is why reading a query value with
// `decodeComponent` is a bug the type system cannot catch and the member
// names have to.
echo Core\Uri::decodeComponent("a+b"), "\n";
echo Core\Uri::decodeFormValue("a+b"), "\n";

// `%20` is a space to both, so a query string written by something that
// followed RFC 3986 still reads through the form decoder.
echo Core\Uri::decodeComponent("a%20b"), "\n";
echo Core\Uri::decodeFormValue("a%20b"), "\n";

// And `%2B` is the `+` the user actually typed.
echo Core\Uri::decodeFormValue("a%2Bb"), "\n";

// Either case of hex reads — RFC 3986 § 6.2.2.1's normalization rule seen
// from the reading side, since `%2f` and `%2F` are one octet.
echo Core\Uri::decodeComponent("%2f%2F"), "\n";

// A `%` that does not begin two hex digits is left exactly as it stands: a
// decoder sits at the edge of a request, where a byte a client mistyped must
// not abort a handler that was going to reject the value anyway.
echo Core\Uri::decodeComponent("%zz-%4-100%"), "\n";

// Each encoder's own output decodes back to what went in, which is the
// property that makes the pair usable at all.
echo Core\Uri::decodeComponent(Core\Uri::encodeComponent("a b/~é")), "\n";
echo Core\Uri::decodeFormValue(Core\Uri::encodeFormValue("a b/~é")), "\n";
--EXPECT--
a+b
a b
a b
a b
a+b
//
%zz-%4-100%
a b/~é
a b/~é
